> >         > > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > I have done this many times and it's easy to do. Not
> >         sure how to do it
> >         > >
> >         > > > in the stan build system however. If this is not
> >         available in the
> >         > >
> >         > > > current build system, I'm afraid you'll need to add this
> >         feature. I
> >         > >
> >         > > > usually work with autotools or cmake.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > We don't have it in yet and we don't use autotools or
> >         cmake. Do you know the mechanism that does it? C macros?
> >         Something else? The problem is that CVODE doesn't include
> >         enough headers to compile unless you run their install
> >         instructions. That means a user needs to install to make all
> >         of Stan compile. That isn't what we want.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > > Things we need to do:
> >         > >
> >         > > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > > 1) tests: unit tests, tests to make sure all generated
> >         ODEs work
> >         > >
> >         > > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > Since my implementation is a drop-in replacement for the
> >         existing
> >         > >
> >         > > > solver, I'm just using the existing unit tests. I did
> >         need to change
> >         > >
> >         > > > some of the thresholds as I'm asking for such an
> >         accurate solution from
> >         > >
> >         > > > CVODE that the tests fail because the FD gradients are
> >         not good as a
> >         > >
> >         > > > reference. This was all checked in matlab with the true
> >         analytic
> >         > >
> >         > > > solutions. Maybe it's a good idea to add this as a unit
> >         test as well,
> >         > >
> >         > > > using the true analytic solution as a reference instead
> >         of the FD
> >         > >
> >         > > > estimate.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > Yes, that would be great to add those as unit tests as
> >         well.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > > 2) doc: at the class level, in the documentation
> >         > >
> >         > > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > The CVODE implementation is a drop-in replacement, so I
> >         kept the
> >         > >
> >         > > > available documentation. I can surely add more
> >         documentation wherever
> >         > >
> >         > > > needed.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > Thanks. Let's start by moving things and we'll take
> >         inventory later.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > > 3) evaluations: how do the different solvers (within
> >         and outside of
> >         > >
> >         > > > > CVODE) compare. Does it actually do a reasonable job
> >         with a stiff
> >         > >
> >         > > > > solver on a non-stiff problem? Should we be looking at
> >         ARKode or LSODA
> >         > >
> >         > > > > instead?
> >         > >
> >         > > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > The underlying algorithms in CVODE are the same as LSODA
> >         ((BDF and
> >         > >
> >         > > > Adams-Moulton). I haven't looked at the LSODA
> >         implementation, but I
> >         > >
> >         > > > suspect that it might have some small numerical
> >         differences. An
> >         > >
> >         > > > interesting aspect of LSODA is that it has heuristics
> >         builtin to switch
> >         > >
> >         > > > automatically between the explicit and implicit methods.
> >         Comparisons are
> >         > >
> >         > > > great, but I fear the results might be too
> >         problem-specific to
> >         > >
> >         > > > generalize. I would maybe even advocate the ability for
> >         the user to
> >         > >
> >         > > > decide on the method used (as well as absolute and
> >         relative tolerances!
> >         > >
> >         > > > ). Using the simulation mode in stan, the user can
> >         investigate which
> >         > >
> >         > > > method gives the best trade-off.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > > Thanks for the info. I didn't realize it was using a
> >         heuristic to go between the two.
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > > >
> >         > >
> >         > >
